            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Reports what a tensor is: its shape, its dtype, the device it lives on, and whether it is tracking gradients - each on its own outlet.\n\nIt is the first thing to patch in when a torch node refuses a tensor or gives a surprising result. The cause is nearly always one of these four: the shape is not what you thought, the type is wrong (integers where floats were expected), it is on a different device from the tensor it meets, or it is carrying gradient history. The outlet labels change to show the values, so the node itself is a display.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.info\n\nEXAMPLE:\nt.info\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin:\nThe tensor. Every tensor that arrives is reported. A list or NumPy array is turned into a float32 cpu tensor first and that is what is reported - so t.info on a NumPy array always says float32, whatever the array's own type. An empty tensor is reported like any other: its shape has a 0 in it ([0] for an empty 1-D tensor). An empty list is reported the same way, as [0].\n\nOUTPUTS (sent right to left - grad first, shape last):\n\nshape:\nThe size of each dimension, as a list: [3, 4] is 3 rows of 4.\n\ndtype:\nThe type, as text: torch.float32, torch.int64 ...\n\ndevice:\nWhere it lives, as text: cpu, or mps:0, cuda:0 for a GPU.\n\ngrad:\nTrue if the tensor tracks gradients, False if not. The outlet's label reads \"grad\" or \"inference\" to match.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nA metro makes a fresh t.rand tensor three times a second; pick a shape from the menu to change it (2x3x4 has three dimensions). The tensor then passes through t.to, whose dtype and device menus you can change, and whose requires_grad box you can tick. Each change appears in the four displays and in the outlet labels.\n\nRELATED:\nt.numel gives the total number of values. t.is_contiguous tells you how the values are laid out in memory. tensor (t.to) changes the dtype, device and requires_grad that this reports. np.shape is the NumPy version of the shape outlet.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 782,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "Reports what a tensor is: its shape, its dtype, the device it lives on, and whether it is tracking gradients - each on its own outlet.\n\nIt is the first thing to patch in when a torch node refuses a tensor or gives a surprising result. The cause is nearly always one of these four: the shape is not what you thought, the type is wrong (integers where floats were expected), it is on a different device from the tensor it meets, or it is carrying gradient history. The outlet labels change to show the values, so the node itself is a display.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.info\n\nEXAMPLE:\nt.info\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin:\nThe tensor. Every tensor that arrives is reported. A list or NumPy array is turned into a float32 cpu tensor first and that is what is reported - so t.info on a NumPy array always says float32, whatever the array's own type. An empty tensor is reported like any other: its shape has a 0 in it ([0] for an empty 1-D tensor). An empty list is reported the same way, as [0].\n\nOUTPUTS (sent right to left - grad first, shape last):\n\nshape:\nThe size of each dimension, as a list: [3, 4] is 3 rows of 4.\n\ndtype:\nThe type, as text: torch.float32, torch.int64 ...\n\ndevice:\nWhere it lives, as text: cpu, or mps:0, cuda:0 for a GPU.\n\ngrad:\nTrue if the tensor tracks gradients, False if not. The outlet's label reads \"grad\" or \"inference\" to match.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nA metro makes a fresh t.rand tensor three times a second; pick a shape from the menu to change it (2x3x4 has three dimensions). The tensor then passes through t.to, whose dtype and device menus you can change, and whose requires_grad box you can tick. Each change appears in the four displays and in the outlet labels.\n\nRELATED:\nt.numel gives the total number of values. t.is_contiguous tells you how the values are laid out in memory. tensor (t.to) changes the dtype, device and requires_grad that this reports. np.shape is the NumPy version of the shape outlet."
        },
